# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the repo AND in the working tree.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so every
# fresh clone on Sandra's Windows PCs checked every text file out with CRLF.
# The committed blobs were already LF, so nothing was wrong in the repo — but
# the many tests that read a source file and match it with \n-anchored regexes
# failed locally while passing in CI on Ubuntu: 39 failures out of 877, across
# 18 files, all of them false. `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf for this repo,
# so a checkout on either PC matches what CI sees.
#
# text=auto still lets git detect binary content, so the generated PWA icons
# (public/icon-v1-*.png) are left byte-for-byte alone — and so is
# test/session-who-when.test.js, which carries NUL bytes in a control-character
# fixture and is therefore auto-detected as binary too.
* text=auto eol=lf
